{
 "thread": "UFg4Af7X5LU",
 "subject": "Changing edge widths on points",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2013-09-05T16:07:04Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "tW338eoR63AJ",
   "from": "Chris Knight",
   "author": "8ad41b4ebc777a42",
   "date": "2013-09-04T11:29:08Z",
   "subject": "Changing edge widths on points",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">While geom_point accepts &#39;colour&#39; and &#39;fill&#39; arguments, which work together for shapes 21:25, it only has a single size argument, which seems to be a synonym for both cex and lwd, which it would be useful to be able to manipulate separately to vary the edge width of these plot characters (in my case I simply want to keep a similar ratio between the edge and centre colours for different sizes of plot character).</font><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">So for example with the data:</font></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">df2 &lt;- data.frame(x = 1:5 , y = 1:25, z=1:5, z2=rep(1:5,each=5))</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I&#39;m looking for a ggplot2 version of this sort of thing from base graphics:</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">plot(y~x, data=df2, col=&quot;Red&quot;, bg=&quot;Black&quot;, pch=23, cex=z, lwd=z2)</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">But if I attempt to do that with geom_point it only changes size, not the line width:</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">library(ggplot2)</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">s </span>&lt;-<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>ggplot(<span style=\"color:#000000\">df2</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>aes(<span style=\"color:#000000\">x </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> x</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> y </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> y</span>))</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">s <span style=\"color:#071a99\">+</span> <span style=\"color:#071a99\">geom_point(aes(</span>cex<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span>z<span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> lwd<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span>z2<span style=\"color:#071a99\">),</span>shape <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#045218\">23</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> colour <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Red&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> fill <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Black&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span> <span style=\"color:#071a99\">+</span></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">  </span>scale_shape_identity()</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font size=\"2\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Am I missing something obvious and/or is there any way to get round this? (and if not could an &#39;edgewidth&#39; argument or similar </font><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">for geom_point</span><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">be added to a wish list?)</span></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">The best bet I&#39;ve come up with so far which gives something of the same effect is this:</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">s </span>+<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>geom_point(aes(<span style=\"color:#000000\">size</span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\">z</span>),<span style=\"color:#000000\">shape </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#045218\">19</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> colour </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Red&quot;</span>)+<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>geom_point(aes(<span style=\"color:#000000\">size</span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\">z2</span>),<span style=\"color:#000000\">shape </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#045218\">20</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> colour </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Black&quot;</span>)<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>+</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">  </span>scale_shape_identity()</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">However, working out appropriate sizes is tricky and it&#39;s not going to work when, downstream, I want to use this with transparency.</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">Thanks,</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">Chris</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></div><p></p><div><br></div><p></p><p></p></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "3MkXAikl7kUJ",
   "from": "Ryan Hope",
   "author": "87cf4991b6a810ee",
   "date": "2013-09-05T16:02:13Z",
   "subject": "Re: Changing edge widths on points",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The problem is that geom_point does not pass lwd on to gpar(), the custom geom below will do what you want:<div><br></div><div><div>GeomPoint2 &lt;- proto(ggplot2:::GeomPoint, {</div><div>  objname &lt;- &quot;point2&quot;</div>\r\n<div>  default_aes &lt;- function(.) aes(shape=16, colour=&quot;black&quot;, size=2, fill = NA, alpha = NA, thickness=1)</div><div>  draw &lt;- function(., data, scales, coordinates, na.rm = FALSE, ...) {    </div><div>    data &lt;- remove_missing(data, na.rm, c(&quot;x&quot;, &quot;y&quot;, &quot;size&quot;, &quot;shape&quot;, &quot;thickness&quot;), name = &quot;geom_point&quot;)</div>\r\n<div>    if (empty(data)) return(zeroGrob())</div><div>    with(coord_transform(coordinates, data, scales), </div><div>         ggname(.$my_name(), pointsGrob(x, y, size=unit(size, &quot;mm&quot;), pch=shape, gp=gpar(col=alpha(colour, alpha), fill = alpha(fill, alpha), lwd = thickness, fontsize = size * .pt)))</div>\r\n<div>    )</div><div>  }</div><div>})</div><div><br></div><div>geom_point2 &lt;- function (mapping = NULL, data = NULL, stat = &quot;identity&quot;, position = &quot;identity&quot;, na.rm = FALSE, ...) {<br></div><div>  GeomPoint2$new(mapping = mapping, data = data, stat = stat, position = position, na.rm = na.rm, ...)</div>\r\n<div>}</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div>ggplot(df2, aes(x=x, y=y)) + geom_point2(aes(size=z, thickness=z2), shape=23, colour=&quot;Red&quot;, fill=&quot;Black&quot;) + scale_shape_identity()</div></div><div>\r\n<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Sep 4, 2013 at 7:29 AM, Chris Knight <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">knig...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">While geom_point accepts &#39;colour&#39; and &#39;fill&#39; arguments, which work together for shapes 21:25, it only has a single size argument, which seems to be a synonym for both cex and lwd, which it would be useful to be able to manipulate separately to vary the edge width of these plot characters (in my case I simply want to keep a similar ratio between the edge and centre colours for different sizes of plot character).</font><div>\r\n<font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">So for example with the data:</font></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">df2 &lt;- data.frame(x = 1:5 , y = 1:25, z=1:5, z2=rep(1:5,each=5))</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">I&#39;m looking for a ggplot2 version of this sort of thing from base graphics:</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">plot(y~x, data=df2, col=&quot;Red&quot;, bg=&quot;Black&quot;, pch=23, cex=z, lwd=z2)</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">But if I attempt to do that with geom_point it only changes size, not the line width:</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">library(ggplot2)</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;line-height:normal\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">\r\n<font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">s </span>&lt;-<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>ggplot(<span style=\"color:#000000\">df2</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> </span>aes(<span style=\"color:#000000\">x </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> x</span>,<span style=\"color:#000000\"> y </span>=<span style=\"color:#000000\"> y</span>))</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">s <span style=\"color:#071a99\">+</span> <span style=\"color:#071a99\">geom_point(aes(</span>cex<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span>z<span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> lwd<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span>z2<span style=\"color:#071a99\">),</span>shape <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#045218\">23</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> colour <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Red&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span> fill <span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span> <span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Black&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span> <span style=\"color:#071a99\">+</span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">  </span>scale_shape_identity()</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">\r\n<font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Am I missing something obvious and/or is there any way to get round this? (and if not could an &#39;edgewidth&#39; argument or similar </font><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">for geom_point</span><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">be added to a wish list?)</span></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">The best bet I&#39;ve come up with so far which gives something of the same effect is this:</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span>s </span>+<span> </span>geom_point(aes(<span>size</span>=<span>z</span>),<span>shape </span>=<span> </span><span style=\"color:#045218\">19</span>,<span> colour </span>=<span> </span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Red&quot;</span>)+<span> </span>geom_point(aes(<span>size</span>=<span>z2</span>),<span>shape </span>=<span> </span><span style=\"color:#045218\">20</span>,<span> colour </span>=<span> </span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;Black&quot;</span>)<span> </span>+</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span>  </span>scale_shape_identity()</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">However, working out appropriate sizes is tricky and it&#39;s not going to work when, downstream, I want to use this with transparency.</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Thanks,</font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\">\r\n<font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Chris</font></p><span><font color=\"#888888\"><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\">\r\n<font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></p><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><p></p><div><br></div><p></p>\r\n<p></p></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>\r\nPlease provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372185648000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Hdb004Nz6WbINRjKGFQoV\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a><br>\r\n <br>\r\nTo post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nTo unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nMore options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372185648000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JwfXrlJko8bCcbxFwhOE8\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a><br>\r\n <br>\r\n--- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372185648000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0W09keASS8ji2ALBoah2UI\">https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br><br clear=\"all\"><div><br></div>-- <br>Ryan Hope, M.S.<br>CogWorks Lab<br>Cognitive Science Department<br>Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute\r\n</div>\r\n"
